1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This is the VOA Special English Economics report. Microsoft has fought legal battles with officials in Europe and the United States over competition in the personal computer market.

2
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
But at the end of March Microsoft accused Google of being anti-competitive.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:34,000
A complaint to the European Commission accused Google of unfairly controlling the internet search market in Europe.

4
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Google was already talking to the Commission about the issue and said it was happy to explain to anyone how its business works.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:56,000
But Google also faced other issues. Gmail users in China began reporting problems with Google's email service in late February.

6
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:07,000
The problems came as news of the revolutions in the Arab world filled the internet and there were online calls for protests in China.

7
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Google said the government was interfering with its email service. Chinese foreign ministry spokeswoman Zhang Yu called those accusations unacceptable.

8
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:31,000
But Google spokeswoman Jessica Powell said this is a government blockage carefully designed to look like the problem is with Gmail.

9
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Google is the world leader in internet search. But in China, the biggest search engine is by do.

10
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:50,000
And in late March China's largest internet media company Sina dropped Google's search engine from its website.

11
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Sina said it would use its own technology.

12
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:04,000
An estimated 450 million Chinese are online about half of all internet users in Asia.

13
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:15,000
In 2010 Google said a cyber attack from China had attempted to get information from the Gmail accounts of human rights activists.

14
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Google also expressed concerns about censorship.

15
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,000
So Google relocated its Chinese search engine from the mainland to Hong Kong.

16
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
In March Google also faced new problems at home.

17
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,000
A federal judge in New York ruled against its plan to put millions of books online.

18
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Google wants to create a digital library of all the world's books.

19
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:53,000
It reached a $125 million deal in 2008 with groups representing writers and publishers.

20
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Google agreed to create a system to pay copyright holders when their works are used online.

21
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,000
But Judge Denny Chen rejected the proposed settlement.

22
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
He said it would give Google monopoly control of the book's search market.

23
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
But he left open the possibility for a new plan.

24
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:26,000
On a similar issue by do in China said it removed almost 3 million documents from its library.

25
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Writers have complained that by do did not have permission for their works to appear on its document sharing site.

26
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,000
For VOA Special English, I'm Alex Villarreal.

27
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:51,000
You can read and listen to our programs about business and other subjects at VOAspecialEnglish.com.

28
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:59,000
